t a new lesson by any means  in fact  it was a huge part of our history and we  lost our connection to it  blaming on the post-60s liberalism which  moved  us away from the culture of aspiration  to the culture of dependency  that defines the black underclass even  to this day  i'm a student of thomas shaw and one of  my favorite stories of his is one that  he tells about dunbar high school in  washington dc  today it is one of the worst schools in  america but that was not always the case  from 1870 that's five years after the  civil war ended to 1955 dunbar was the  best all-black public school in d.c  by 1899 the school had made so much  progress despite slavery not being that  long ago and despite segregation being  the law of the land the school made so  much progress that since 1899 dunbar  students were coming in first in  city-wide test-giving in both black and  white schools  seoul wrote that many of these dunbar  graduates  could only afford to attend  low-cost colleges such as howard  university at that time in minor  teachers college  however that were done by graduates who  attended harvard amherst overland and  many other great colleges  these students ran up impressive records  of academic success and honors  the first black general the the  discovery of blood plasma the first  black senator since reconstruction and  many more  all graduates of dunbar  then the school fell apart  in 1954 brown vs board of education  ruled that racially separated schools  were inherently unequal  in the south there was no question that  blacks were providing an adequate  inadequate education but that was not  the case of dunbar  what made the school so successful was  that  all of the aspiration and the  aspirational blacks from all over dc  could apply  it didn't matter where you lived in that  city  they had their own former school choice  and could apply to dunbar  where they could be with other  like-minded aspirational people  the unintended consequences of brown  versus the board of education was that  it turned dunbar into a neighborhood  school  very much like what we see all across  america today  all of a sudden the many blacks in dc no  longer had the choice to apply to dunbar  and you know what happened it collapsed  i think that story by seoul is so  relevant today  i've said before on earlier rooftop  revelations that we cannot save  everybody we just can't  that's not true for just blacks but for  all of america  but what we can do  today is awaken the spirit of aspiration  especially among the poor  you do that by giving them opportunities  or at the least pathways to  opportunities  and that is why i'm such a huge believer  in school  it is in my opinion the civil rights  issue of our time  and that is why i'm fighting so hard  against the politicians in my city and  state to remove the moratorium against  charter schools so that we can have  one right in project hood our community  center needs it  the glory days of dunbar may be long  gone  but its lessons are not  they are timeless  especially the lesson of aspiring to be  better  thomas saul  is the biggest advocate for school  choice today and it's an honor to follow  the path as we seek to transform the  south side for  better to be more aspirational  join me in my journey join me in the  struggle join me in the fight  as we choose school choice  over everything  as i always say  god bless you  and until tomorrow  peace  you
 

